<p>A denial letter can stop you in your tracks. If your <strong>hail claim was denied in Texas</strong>, you still have options, but the right next step depends on why the claim was denied and which insurer issued the decision. Many denials rest on a short inspection, a disagreement about when the damage happened, or missing documentation. Those are problems that better evidence can sometimes address.</p>

<p>This guide is written for homeowners and business owners in Corpus Christi and the Coastal Bend, where hail claims are often handled by the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) rather than a standard homeowners carrier. The process, and the deadlines, are different for each.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Claim-Was-Denied-in-Texas-2-1024x683.webp" alt="Homeowner pointing to printed hail damage photos while a claims professional reviews a denial letter and binder" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Start with the letter itself. The stated reason for denial shapes everything you do next.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Why hail claims get denied in Texas</h2>

<p>Denials tend to follow a few patterns. Knowing which one applies to you tells you what evidence will matter.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stated reason</th><th>What it usually means</th><th>Evidence that can respond to it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>"No storm-created damage found"</td><td>The adjuster did not see or did not credit hail impacts</td><td>A second inspection, chalk-circled close-ups with scale, dented soft metals, test-square counts per slope</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wear and tear, age, or deterioration</td><td>The insurer attributes the condition to aging, not a sudden event</td><td>Photos showing fresh fractures or bruising, granule loss patterns consistent with impact, roof age records</td></tr>
<tr><td>Damage predates the policy or the reported storm</td><td>A dispute over the date of loss</td><td>NOAA storm reports for your date, dated photos, prior inspection reports, neighbors’ claims on the same date</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cosmetic damage only</td><td>Your policy may exclude cosmetic damage, often on metal roofing</td><td>The exclusion wording, evidence of functional damage such as cracked seams, leaks, or compromised coatings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Late notice</td><td>The insurer says the claim was not reported promptly</td><td>Your timeline of discovery and reporting; for TWIA, the one-year filing rule in §2210.573</td></tr>
<tr><td>Below the deductible</td><td>The insurer’s estimate is lower than your wind/hail deductible</td><td>A detailed independent estimate that captures missed items</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Notice that most of these are disputes about evidence, not about the policy. That is why the response usually starts with better documentation.</p>

<h2>First steps after a denial</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Get the decision in writing.</strong> If the denial came by phone, ask for a written letter that states the reason and cites the policy language.</li>
<li><strong>Request the insurer’s file items.</strong> Ask for the adjuster’s estimate, photos, and any engineer or inspection report the decision relied on.</li>
<li><strong>Read your policy.</strong> Look at the declarations page, the wind/hail deductible, any cosmetic damage exclusion, the duties-after-loss section, and the appraisal clause.</li>
<li><strong>Confirm which insurer covers hail.</strong> TWIA covers wind and hail for eligible properties in Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties. If you have a TWIA policy, your homeowners carrier may have denied the claim simply because its policy excludes wind and hail. In that case the claim may belong with TWIA.</li>
<li><strong>Write down every deadline.</strong> Use our <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> as a starting point and verify dates against your policy.</li>
</ol>

<h2>TWIA hail denials versus private insurer denials</h2>

<p>Texas law sets different rules for TWIA and for private insurers. Mixing them up can cost you time.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Issue</th><th>TWIA (Tex. Ins. Code Ch. 2210, Subch. L-1)</th><th>Private insurer (Ch. 542 and 542A)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Decision deadline</td><td>60 days after receiving the claim or requested information (§2210.573)</td><td>15 business days after receiving all requested items, or 45 days after notice that more time is needed (§542.056)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dispute over amount</td><td>Demand appraisal within 60 days of the accept or partial-accept notice (§2210.574)</td><td>Appraisal clause in the policy, if it has one</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dispute over coverage denial</td><td>Notice of intent to sue before limitations runs; TWIA may require mediation or a moderated settlement conference (§2210.575)</td><td>Written pre-suit notice at least 61 days before filing suit (§542A.003)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deadline to sue</td><td>No later than two years after receiving the denial notice (§2210.577)</td><td>Set by Texas law and policy terms; ask a Texas attorney</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extra help</td><td>TDI ombudsman program for TWIA policyholders (§2210.582)</td><td>TDI consumer complaints</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Chapter 542A’s pre-suit notice rule does not apply to suits against TWIA (§542A.002(b)). Because these are legal deadlines, treat the table as a map, not legal advice. If a lawsuit is on the table, consult a Texas attorney. Our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page covers the TWIA process in more depth.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Claim-Was-Denied-in-Texas-3-1024x683.webp" alt="Two men on a shingle roof at sunset reviewing an estimate on a clipboard with a calculator" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A second, careful inspection often finds damage that a quick first visit missed or did not credit.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Get a second inspection</h2>

<p>Initial inspections after a big hail day are often fast. Adjusters may be working dozens of claims at once, and some damage is subtle. A second look by a qualified roofer, an independent inspector, or a licensed public adjuster can change the picture.</p>

<p>A careful re-inspection should look at:</p>
<ul>
<li>Each roof slope, with impacts marked and counted in a defined test area.</li>
<li>Soft metals: gutters, downspouts, vent caps, flashing, and the AC condenser.</li>
<li>Ridge caps and hip shingles, which often show impacts clearly.</li>
<li>Siding, stucco, fiber cement, windows, and screens on each elevation.</li>
<li>The attic, for wet decking, stains, or light through the roof.</li>
</ul>

<p>Salt air and age complicate things on the coast. Corrosion on metal components near the bays can look like pre-existing damage, and insurers will point to it. Good documentation separates new impact damage from older wear rather than ignoring the old wear.</p>

<h2>Connect the damage to a storm date</h2>

<p>Date-of-loss disputes are common. NOAA’s Storm Events Database can help. Nueces County hail is concentrated in spring: 86 of the county’s 134 NOAA hail reports fell in April or May. Specific reports can be powerful, such as the <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=506535" target="_blank" rel="noopener">April 4, 2014 Banquete report</a> of a house damaged by golf ball hail, or the <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=368459" target="_blank" rel="noopener">March 29, 2012 report</a> of golf ball hail just west of Robstown. Use our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> to search near your address, and see our guide on <a href="/document-hail-damage-for-an-insurance-claim/">how to document hail damage for an insurance claim</a> for a full evidence checklist.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Ask for reconsideration or submit a supplement</h2>

<p>Once you have stronger evidence, put it in front of the insurer in writing.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Usual response</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Claim denied, but new evidence shows covered hail damage</td><td>Written request for reconsideration with the new inspection, photos, and storm data</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claim paid, but the estimate left out items</td><td>A supplement with line items, photos, and pricing for what was missed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claim paid, and the parties disagree on the amount</td><td>Negotiation, then appraisal if the policy or statute provides it</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>A reconsideration package usually includes a short cover letter referencing the claim number and the denial reason, the re-inspection report, organized photos by slope and elevation, the NOAA report for your date, and a detailed repair estimate. If parts of the claim were paid but items were missed, our guide to <a href="/hail-damage-claim-supplements-what-carriers-miss-in-their-scope/">hail damage claim supplements</a> explains how to document those items.</p>

<h2>What to put in a reconsideration letter</h2>

<p>A short, organized letter is easier for a claims reviewer to act on than a long complaint. Keep it factual and attach the proof.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Header:</strong> your name, the property address, the policy number, the claim number, and the date of loss.</li>
<li><strong>The denial reason:</strong> quote the sentence from the denial letter you are responding to.</li>
<li><strong>What is new:</strong> list the new evidence, such as the re-inspection date and who performed it, the NOAA report for your date, and photo sets by slope.</li>
<li><strong>What you are asking for:</strong> a re-inspection, a revised estimate, or reconsideration of the coverage decision, stated plainly.</li>
<li><strong>Attachments:</strong> numbered and referenced in the letter so the reviewer can find each item.</li>
</ul>

<p>Send it in a way you can prove, such as email with the claim number in the subject line or certified mail, and note the date in your claim log. If the insurer asks for more information, respond in writing. For private insurers, the Chapter 542 decision clock generally runs from receipt of all the items the insurer requested, so prompt, complete answers keep the claim moving.</p>

<h2>Appraisal, complaints, and legal options</h2>

<p><strong>Appraisal.</strong> If the insurer accepts that hail damage exists but you disagree about the cost, appraisal may be available. Each side picks an appraiser, the appraisers pick an umpire, and an award agreed by any two is binding on the amount, subject to the policy language. Appraisal does not decide coverage. For TWIA, the demand must come within 60 days of the acceptance notice, and the costs are split equally. Learn more on our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page.</p>

<p><strong>TDI complaint.</strong> The Texas Department of Insurance accepts complaints about insurer conduct, and its ombudsman program helps TWIA policyholders.</p>

<p><strong>Legal action.</strong> Lawsuits, bad faith questions, and deadlines to sue are legal matters. A public adjuster cannot give legal advice. Talk with a Texas attorney who handles insurance disputes, and do it early enough to protect the deadlines in the table above.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Claim-Was-Denied-in-Texas-4-1024x683.webp" alt="Inspector writing notes beside asphalt shingles with multiple circular hail impacts and granule loss" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Clear impact marks with granule loss, photographed slope by slope, are the kind of evidence that answers a "no hail damage found" denial.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Mistakes that make a denial harder to overturn</h2>

<ul>
<li>Accepting a phone denial without asking for the reason in writing.</li>
<li>Replacing the roof before anyone documents the damage for the insurer.</li>
<li>Letting the TWIA one-year filing window or the 60-day appraisal window pass.</li>
<li>Sending a reconsideration request with no new evidence.</li>
<li>Arguing by phone without a written record of who said what and when.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When to call a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster</h2>

<p>A licensed public adjuster represents you, not the insurer. After a denial, a public adjuster can review the letter and policy, re-inspect the property, rebuild the documentation, prepare an estimate, and present the claim for reconsideration. It may make sense when the damage is significant, the denial reason is evidence-based, or the claim involves both TWIA and another carrier. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<p>If you are weighing your options, our comparison of a <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster-for-hail-claims-in-texas/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster for hail claims in Texas</a> explains the difference in roles, and our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page explains how we approach these files. There is no promise of a different outcome, but an honest second opinion costs nothing. As a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster serving Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and surrounding Coastal Bend communities, we offer a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a>. Call or text [[phone]].</p>
